#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173194 :tutorial/content "Note the following facts regarding slope fields:\n\n- Recall that for a differential equation of the form $y' = f(x),$ the slope field has *vertical* symmetry.\n\n- Also recall that for a differential equation of the form $y' = g(y),$ the slope field has *horizontal* symmetry.\n\n- In contrast, a differential equation of the form $y' = f(x,y)$ usually has neither vertical nor horizontal symmetry.\n\nWe can use these facts to determine the type of equation that generates a particular slope field. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "03296d2b-05de-5077-8eab-c619925af706" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5668 :tutorial/title "Symmetry in Slope Fields"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175693} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050718}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512082} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619728}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175694} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511981} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619717}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176057 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275434}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177064 :tutorial/content "Let's look again at the graph of the function $y=\\cos x.$ \n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nThe cosine function has infinitely many zeros and an infinite number of points where the function reaches its maximum and minimum value. Let's write general expressions for these properties, assuming $n$ is an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the zeros of the function are $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\dots\n$$ \n\nA general expression for the zeros is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Maxima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a maximum when $x=0$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that maximize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Minima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a minimum when $x=\\pi$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that minimize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = \\pi + 2n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46e10740-fb99-59c2-8277-45bf39d4988e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6896 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177065 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of the cosine function again.\n\n![](images/04/04f13f2dec860874174f51990f4260615e7be893a6be5f86b3b13857c4e8953f.png)\n\nThe periodicity of the function is another characteristic of its graph. The period of the cosine function is $2\\pi,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/78/789fa06939a3e3350a18c4e9cdf078bf6baf3f025709703e608cb3b7ba89e698.png)\n\nThis means that $\\cos\\left( x+2n\\pi\\right) = \\cos \\left( x\\right)$ for any number $x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5cc2e21c-1a75-53dd-85be-61d9124f25c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6897 :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177082 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *even* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosine is an even function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos(x).\n$$\n\nEven functions are always symmetric about the $y$-axis. This is indeed true of the cosine function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/b1/b1f44126ba6188d147026b6fe5a4066b0051ec3b638bb2d3e34ee1cbb1708cae.png)\n\nWe can use the evenness property of cosine in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ca8a70-b8fa-5e67-adec-ee263651a473" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7897 :tutorial/title "The Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179557} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "589c680e-9f93-58e9-9dab-c34de98d7225" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323697}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Half-Life Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179806 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179528} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049854}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321140}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Radioactive Decay Given the Half-Life of a Substance"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179807 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179556} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383494}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Radioactive Dating to Estimate the Age of an Object"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180679 :tutorial/content "Some substances emit **radiation,** meaning that they release energy, often in the form of small particles, into the surrounding environment. If a substance emits radiation, it is **radioactive**. \n\nSince radioactive substances are continually losing particles to the surrounding environment, the mass of a radioactive substance will decrease over time.  \n\nThe mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive sample is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, $M_0$ is the initial mass of the sample, and the constant $k$ is dependent upon the type of substance.\n\nThe general solution to this initial value problem is \n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nA graph showing how the mass of a typical radioactive substance varies with time is shown below.\n\n![](images/07/0727db8a9f3c955b6c3306f12f1594b00a93085235a42f19607f4d434033f7c0.png)\n\nEvery type of radioactive substance has a value of $k$ associated with it. However, rather than working with $k,$ physicists and chemists tend to prefer working with the **half-life** of a substance. The half-life of a particular substance is the time it takes for a sample of that substance to decrease by a factor of one-half. \n\nThe half-life of a substance is denoted $t_{1/2},$ and is the time indicated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab1a2143e190024710dc391cddd7c9de0d486c5c4b8d055fbd4e5c2701ba0803.png)\n\nThe half-life also tells us how long it takes for the mass of the substance to halve once again. \n\n![](images/eb/eb2bb2b6cac61ba9eea337e79e6d268e22eb9c50c23d43a74c95637433c0a658.png)\n\nAnd again.\n\n![](images/59/595d609c7c8b6c86fb0bd5ac21f82f2d91ac03e2fbddcb70cf6ba72e216edd11.png)\n\nThis pattern of continually halving in mass continues indefinitely." :tutorial/id #uuid "022a510f-6dfa-5ddc-a2a4-dfc6734f8f8c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4073 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180691 :tutorial/content "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance is given by\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life of a substance is the time taken for the mass of a sample to reduce by a factor of one-half. Therefore, it is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12 M_0 = M_0 e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-kt} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{-kt}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}1-\\operatorname{ln}2 & =-kt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}2 & =-kt \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}2}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the half-life of a substance is given by the formula\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nEvery radioactive substance has a half-life associated with it. It depends only on the substance itself and is independent of its initial mass of the sample (we know this because we were able to cancel $M_0$ in our derivation of $t_{1/2}$). \n\nIt's worth noting that half-lives show significant variation in magnitude across different substances. Some substances that decay very rapidly have half-lives on the order of nanoseconds. On the other hand, some substances that decay very slowly have half-lives that are on the order of billions of years." :tutorial/id #uuid "cda969b3-51ce-59ee-9fc7-21187fd87887" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5721 :tutorial/title "Deriving a Formula for the Half-Life"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184057} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694965} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694963}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184058} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a58c0a18-4221-50f1-b31c-ae58d099d79d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694479} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694478}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum and Constant Factor Rules"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184059} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "007f3355-427a-5da4-8af7-22581f1cb1de" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694608} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694602}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sums of Power Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185366 :tutorial/content "How can we calculate the integral of a sum of functions, like $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+x^3)\\,\\textrm d x?\n$$ \n\nAccording to the **sum rule**, the integral of the sum of functions is equal to the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\int (f(x) \\pm g(x))\\,\\textrm d x = \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm \\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1})+(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we renamed $c_1+c_2=C.$ (The sum of two arbitrary constants is itself an arbitrary constant.)" :tutorial/id #uuid "207bbd12-158a-58be-b000-82cace6dcdde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 701 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185367 :tutorial/content "For more complicated functions consisting of the sum of functions multiplied by constants, we can combine the constant factor and sum rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int k_1 f(x) \\pm k_2 g(x)\\,\\textrm d x = k_1\\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm k_2\\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b5ce4c9-ef44-52d9-a506-96aa15055e00" :tutorial/math-academy-id 703 :tutorial/title "Combining the Constant Factor and Sum Rules"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228949} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1403309b-bb10-5ce3-bd8f-97915bb837ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672875} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672868}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model: Inflow Equals Outflow"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229182 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228930} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ab1d837-f782-5439-8e25-3c764f9cb5c5" :knowle